                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Mosaic is a game that excels at creating a mood. Its art style is beautiful in a bleak, almost dreamlike way, and the quiet atmosphere does a great job of conveying the monotony and emptiness of everyday life. The world feels dull and lifeless in a way that fits its themes perfectly, and I appreciate how much the game relies on visuals and atmosphere rather than constant dialogue or action.\n\nHowever, I have mixed feelings about the story and some of its underlying ideas.\n\nI understand the message about escaping a meaningless routine and refusing to spend your entire life doing something you hate, but the way the game approaches this idea feels a little too simplistic. [b]You can't just quit a job you hate, ignore your bills, and expect everything to work itself out.[/b] Real life doesn't stop demanding things from you just because you've decided you're tired of it. Financial obligations, responsibilities, and the consequences of your choices don't magically disappear. Escaping the rat race sounds great in theory, but the story doesn't do enough to acknowledge the practical reality of doing so. It feels more like a fantasy of running away from your problems than a meaningful exploration of how to actually confront them.\n\nThe controls are another weak point. They feel clunky and unnecessarily restrictive at times, making movement and interaction more frustrating than they need to be. I understand that the limited controls might be an intentional design choice meant to reinforce the feeling of being trapped in a monotonous routine, but there's a difference between making the player feel powerless and making the gameplay itself feel awkward. An artistic intention doesn't automatically make an inconvenient control system enjoyable.\n\nOverall, Mosaic is an interesting experience with a strong visual identity and a memorable atmosphere, but its storytelling feels somewhat naive, and its controls can get in the way of the experience. I appreciate what the game is trying to say, even if I'm not entirely convinced by how it says it.\n",
